7bc788d1-f746-48f1-956a-c56c80652cf2	67f0d80b-81ed-4f14-8dcb-35705b466da1	Assume that wesen company wishes to prepare an income statement for the month of January when it's records show net sales 400,000 beginning inventory br 40,000 and cost of goods purchase br 200,000 the preceding year the company realized a 25%gross profit rate and it expects to earn the same rate this year required given these facts and assumption the estimated cost of the ending inventory at January 31 under gross profit method?	To calculate the estimated cost of the ending inventory at January 31 using the gross profit method, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the gross profit rate:\n   Gross Profit Rate = Gross Profit / Net Sales\n\n   Since the company expects to earn the same gross profit rate as the preceding year, which is 25%, we can calculate the gross profit as follows:\n   Gross Profit = Net Sales * Gross Profit Rate\n   Gross Profit = 400,000 * 0.25\n   Gross Profit = 100,000\n\n2. Calculate the cost of goods sold:\n   Cost of Goods Sold = Net Sales - Gross Profit\n   Cost of Goods Sold = 400,000 - 100,000\n   Cost of Goods Sold = 300,000\n\n3. Calculate the cost of goods available for sale:\n   Cost of Goods Available for Sale = Beginning Inventory + Cost of Goods Purchased\n   Cost of Goods Available for Sale = 40,000 + 200,000\n   Cost of Goods Available for Sale = 240,000\n\n4. Calculate the estimated ending inventory:\n   Estimated Ending Inventory = Cost of Goods Available for Sale - Cost of Goods Sold\n   Estimated Ending Inventory = 240,000 - 300,000\n   Estimated Ending Inventory = -60,000\n\nBased on the calculations, the estimated cost of the ending inventory at January 31 under the gross profit method is -60,000. However, this result indicates a negative value, which is not possible for inventory. Please double-check the provided information and calculations to ensure accuracy.	439
