44e448d8-5441-45cd-9890-3950b56e7090	4ddcca44-c161-4e98-9a4f-f6eaba7b28d8	What it is the reason of Blood in the sputum and excessive cough	I'm not a doctor, but I can provide some general information. Blood in the sputum, also known as hemoptysis, can be caused by various factors. Some possible reasons for blood in the sputum and excessive coughing include:\n\n1. Respiratory Infections: Conditions like bronchitis, pneumonia, or tuberculosis can cause irritation and inflammation in the airways, leading to coughing up blood.\n\n2. Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD): COPD, including conditions like chronic bronchitis and emphysema, can cause damage to the airways, making them more prone to bleeding.\n\n3. Lung Cancer: Coughing up blood can be a symptom of lung cancer, especially in advanced stages. However, there are many other causes for hemoptysis, so it's important not to jump to conclusions.\n\n4. Pulmonary Embolism: A blood clot in the lungs can cause coughing up blood, along with symptoms like shortness of breath and chest pain.\n\n5. Trauma or Injury: Any injury to the chest or lungs, such as a rib fracture or punctured lung, can result in blood in the sputum.\n\n6. Other Causes: Other potential causes include certain medications, blood clotting disorders, pulmonary hypertension, or even nosebleeds that drip down the throat.\n\nIt's crucial to consult a healthcare professional if you're experiencing blood in the sputum and excessive coughing. They can evaluate your symptoms, perform necessary tests, and provide an accurate diagnosis and appropriate treatment.	358
