, TSS, COD, BOD, and heavy metal concentrations. Standard laboratory methods will be employed for accurate analysis.\n3. Pollutant Identification: Advanced analytical techniques, such as high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC), gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS), and atomic absorption spectroscopy (AAS), will be utilized to identify and quantify the major pollutants present in the textile wastewater.\n4. Environmental Impact Assessment: The potential environmental impacts of textile wastewater discharge will be assessed through field surveys and laboratory analysis of water, soil, and vegetation samples collected from the surrounding areas. This will help in understanding the extent of pollution and its effects on the ecosystem.\n5. Recommendations: Based on the findings of the characterization study, recommendations will be provided for the implementation of effective wastewater treatment and management strategies in Hwassa Industrial Park. This may include the adoption of advanced treatment technologies, implementation of strict regulatory measures, and promotion of sustainable practices.\n\nTimeline and Budget:\nThe proposed study is estimated to be completed within a timeframe of six months. The budget required for this project will depend on various factors, including the number of sampling points, laboratory analysis costs, and field surveys. A detailed budget breakdown will be provided upon approval of this proposal.\n\nConclusion:\nCharacterizing textile wastewater in Hwassa Industrial Park is essential for understanding its composition and potential environmental impacts. This proposal outlines a comprehensive plan to analyze the physicochemical parameters, identify pollutants, assess environmental impacts, and provide recommendations for effective wastewater treatment and management strategies. By implementing the findings and recommendations of this study, Hwassa Industrial Park can mitigate the environmental risks associated with textile 