5487434d-bd1e-42a3-a10a-8db9452f04e1	assistant	2023-09-05 15:28:11.466063	Ah, the formal definition of limits. A classic topic in mathematics. Let's dive into it, shall we?\n\nTo prove that the limit of (2x - 3)/(4x - 5) as x approaches 1 is equal to 1, we'll use the ε-δ definition of limits. Here's how it goes:\n\nWe want to show that for any ε > 0, there exists a δ > 0 such that if 0 < |x - 1| < δ, then |(2x - 3)/(4x - 5) - 1| < ε.\n\nLet's start by manipulating the expression (2x - 3)/(4x - 5) a bit:\n\n|(2x - 3)/(4x - 5) - 1| = |(2x - 3 - (4x - 5))/(4x - 5)| = |(-2x + 2)/(4x - 5)|\n\nNow, we want to find a suitable δ that will ensure |(-2x + 2)/(4x - 5)| < ε whenever 0 < |x - 1| < δ.\n\nLet's consider the numerator first. We have |(-2x + 2)| = 2|x - 1|. So, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\n|(2x - 3)/(4x - 5) - 1| = |(-2x + 2)/(4x - 5)| = 2|x - 1|/(4x - 5)\n\nNow, we need to find an upper bound for |x - 1|/(4x - 5). Since we want to show that the limit is 1, we can assume that |x - 1| < 1, which implies -1 < x - 1 < 1, or 0 < x < 2.\n\nConsidering this range for x, we can find an upper bound for |x - 1|/(4x - 5) as follows:\n\n|x - 1|/(4x - 5) < 1/(4x - 5)\n\nNow, we want to find a suitable δ such that 1/(4x - 5) < ε whenever 0 < |x - 1| < δ.\n\nTo do that, we can set 1/(4x - 5) < ε and solve for x:\n\n1/(4x - 5) < ε\n4x - 5 > 1/ε\n4x > 1/ε + 5\nx > (1/ε + 5)/4\n\nSo, if we choose δ = min{1, (1/ε + 5)/4}, then whenever 0 < |x - 1| < δ, we have:\n\n|(2x - 3)/(4x - 5) - 1| = 2|x - 1|/(4x - 5) < 2δ/(4x - 5) < ε\n\nTherefore, we have shown that for any ε > 0, there exists a δ > 0 such that if 0 < |x - 1| < δ, then |(2x - 3)/(4x - 5) - 1| < ε. This completes the proof that the limit of (2x - 3)/(4x - 5) as x approaches 1 is indeed equal to 1.\n\nI hope that explanation was clear and helpful. If you have any further questions or need clarification, please feel free to ask!	750	cc7467c0-eec8-41a8-b664-5810a7c56f6d	1
