a6c97fb3-f3b2-40d6-af23-cf11cba86e2a	assistant	2023-09-18 05:06:52.082227	One advantage that a portfolio manager obtains by calculating both expected market premiums and expected currency premiums in formulating a global investment strategy is the ability to assess the potential risk and return of investments in different markets.\n\nExpected market premiums refer to the additional return that an investor expects to earn by investing in a particular market compared to a risk-free rate of return. By calculating expected market premiums, a portfolio manager can identify markets that are expected to outperform or underperform relative to others. This information helps in determining the allocation of investments across different markets, allowing the portfolio manager to focus on markets that offer higher potential returns.\n\nExpected currency premiums, on the other hand, refer to the additional return that an investor expects to earn by investing in a particular currency compared to a risk-free rate of return in their home currency. By calculating expected currency premiums, a portfolio manager can assess the potential impact of currency movements on investment returns. This information helps in determining the allocation of investments in different currencies, allowing the portfolio manager to take advantage of favorable currency movements and hedge against unfavorable currency movements.\n\nBy considering both expected market premiums and expected currency premiums, a portfolio manager can construct a global investment strategy that takes into account the potential risk and return of investments in different markets and currencies. This holistic approach helps in diversifying the portfolio and managing risk effectively. It also allows the portfolio manager to identify opportunities for higher returns and make informed decisions regarding currency exposure.\n\nOverall, calculating both expected market premiums and expected currency premiums provides valuable insight