a38863f1-bc39-4c40-a7aa-c0fbb01c5bb5	assistant	2023-09-18 05:12:53.524679	Comparative Short and Precise Notes on Auditing in Ethiopia and the United States:\n\ni. Ethiopian code of professional ethics for auditors:\n\nEthiopia:\n- The Ethiopian code of professional ethics for auditors is governed by the Accounting and Auditing Board of Ethiopia (AABE).\n- The code emphasizes independence, integrity, objectivity, confidentiality, and professional competence as key ethical principles for auditors.\n- It provides guidelines on professional conduct, including the avoidance of conflicts of interest, maintaining professional skepticism, and ensuring the confidentiality of client information.\n- The code also outlines the responsibilities of auditors in terms of professional competence, due care, and compliance with auditing standards.\n\nUnited States:\n- In the United States, auditors are governed by the American Institute of Certified Public Accountants (AICPA) and the Public Company Accounting Oversight Board (PCAOB).\n- The AICPA's code of professional conduct sets forth ethical principles and rules for auditors, including independence, integrity, objectivity, and professional competence.\n- The PCAOB establishes auditing and ethical standards for auditors of public companies, with a focus on independence, due professional care, and adherence to auditing standards.\n- Both the AICPA and PCAOB codes emphasize the importance of maintaining public trust and confidence in the auditing profession.\n\nObjective Critics:\n- Critics of the Ethiopian code argue that it lacks specificity and enforcement mechanisms, leading to potential ethical lapses by auditors.\n- Critics of the U.S. codes argue that they are overly complex and can be subject to interpretation, potentially leading to inconsistencies in ethical practices.\n\nii. Objectives, authority & duties of the Federal Auditor General:\n\nEthiopia:\n- The Federal Auditor General (FAG) is responsible for auditing the accounts of federal government entities in Ethiopia.\n- The objectives of the FAG include ensuring accountability, transparency, and the proper use of public resources.\n- The FAG has the authority to access financial records, conduct audits, and issue audit reports.\n- Duties of the FAG include examining financial statements, assessing compliance with laws and regulations, and making recommendations for improvement.\n\nUnited States:\n- In the United States, the Government Accountability Office (GAO) serves as the federal auditor.\n- The GAO's objectives include promoting accountability, transparency, and efficiency in government operations.\n- The GAO has the authority to access financial records, conduct performance audits, and issue audit reports.\n- Duties of the GAO include evaluating the effectiveness of government programs, assessing compliance with laws and regulations, and providing recommendations for improvement.\n\nObjective Critics:\n- Critics of the Ethiopian system argue that the FAG's authority is limited, leading to potential challenges in enforcing audit findings and recommendations.\n- Critics of the U.S. system argue that the GAO's independence may be compromised due to political influences and funding constraints.\n\niii. Auditing procedures & period limitation of the office:\n\nEthiopia:\n- Auditing procedures in Ethiopia follow international auditing standards, including planning, risk assessment, testing, and reporting.\n- The period limitation for the office of the FAG is not explicitly specified in available sources.\n\nUnited States:\n- Auditing procedures in the United States follow generally accepted auditing standards (GAAS), including planning, risk assessment, testing, and reporting.\n- The period limitation for the GAO's office is typically within a fiscal year, but specific audits may have different timeframes based on the scope and complexity of the audit.\n\nObjective Critics:\n- Critics of the Ethiopian system argue that there may be inconsistencies in the application of auditing procedures due to limited resources and capacity.\n- Critics of the U.S. system argue that the period limitation for audits may not allow for a comprehensive assessment of government operations and may result in limited accountability.\n\nIdentify offenses and penalties that can be imposed by the Federal Auditor General:\n\nEthiopia:\n- Offenses that can be identified by the Federal Auditor General include misappropriation of public funds, fraud, corruption, and non-compliance with financial regulations.\n- Penalties for such offenses can include fines, imprisonment, restitution, and administrative sanctions.\n\nUnited States:\n- Offenses that can be identified by the GAO include fraud, waste, abuse, and mismanagement of federal funds.\n- Penalties for such offenses can include fines, imprisonment, restitution, and administrative sanctions.\n\nObjective Critics:\n- Critics of both systems argue that the enforcement of penalties may be inconsistent and that there may be challenges in holding individuals accountable for offenses identified by the auditors.	899	a3d084ea-d1e1-4918-8c4f-1219e0d10650	1
