model, and potential follow-on funding.\n   - Disadvantages include loss of control, potential conflicts with investors, and high expectations for growth and profitability.\n\n4. Angel Investors:\n   - Angel investors are individuals who provide capital to startups or early-stage companies in exchange for equity ownership.\n   - Angel investors are typically high-net-worth individuals who invest their own funds and often provide mentorship and guidance to the company.\n   - Angel investment is often sought by companies that are too small or risky for traditional financing but have growth potential.\n   - Advantages of angel investment include access to capital, expertise, and networks, as well as flexibility in deal terms.\n   - Disadvantages include loss of control, potential conflicts with investors, and limited availability of angel investors.\n\n5. Crowdfunding:\n   - Crowdfunding involves raising funds from a large number of individuals (the crowd) through online platforms.\n   - Crowdfunding can take different forms, such as donation-based (no financial return), reward-based (non-financial rewards), or equity-based (equity ownership).\n   - Crowdfunding is often used by startups, creative projects, or social enterprises to raise capital and engage with a broader community.\n   - Advantages of crowdfunding include access to a large pool of potential investors, market validation, and marketing and promotion opportunities.\n   - Disadvantages include the need for a compelling campaign, competition for attention, and potential legal and regulatory complexities.\n\n6. Retained Earnings:\n   - Retained earnings are profits that a company has earned and retained for reinvestment in the business.\n   - Instead of distributing profits as dividends, companies can choose to retain earnings to fund growth, expansion, or other investment opportunities.\n   - Retained earnings provide an internal source of finance and can be used for various purposes, such as research and d