l Decisions: These decisions are made by operational managers and focus on day-to-day activities. They are typically routine and repetitive, involving short-term planning and control. Examples include scheduling shifts, ordering inventory, and resolving customer complaints.\n\n2. Tactical Decisions: These decisions are made by tactical managers and involve medium-term planning and resource allocation. They aim to achieve the organization's strategic objectives and optimize performance. Examples include setting sales targets, allocating budgets, and developing marketing campaigns.\n\n3. Strategic Decisions: These decisions are made by strategic managers and have a long-term impact on the organization. They involve setting the overall direction, goals, and policies of the organization. Examples include entering new markets, diversifying product lines, and adopting new technologies.\n\nTo illustrate this, let's consider a manufacturing company:\n\n- An operational manager on the shop floor needs real-time information on production rates, quality control, and machine maintenance to make decisions about production scheduling, resource allocation, and quality improvement.\n\n- A tactical manager in the production department needs summarized information on production costs, inventory levels, and supplier performance to make decisions about capacity planning, cost reduction initiatives, and supplier selection.\n\n- A strategic manager at the corporate level needs information on market trends, competitor analysis, and financial performance to make decisions about market expansion, product diversification, and strategic partnerships.\n\nIn summary, different levels of managers require different types of information for decision-making because their roles, responsibilities, and decision-making contexts vary. Operational managers need detailed, real-time information for day-to-day operations, tactical managers need summarized information for medium-term planning and resource allocation, and strategic managers need high-level, strategic information for long-term planning and goal setting. By providing the right information to the right level of management, organizations can support effective decision-making at all levels.	680	52f20d9f-5fa7-4c44-ab91-8b2bf7bb614e	1
