o make decisions about market entry, mergers and acquisitions, and long-term business strategies.\n\nThe types of information needed by different levels of managers can be categorized into three main categories:\n\n1. Operational Information: This type of information is detailed, specific, and focuses on day-to-day operations. It is used by operational managers to monitor and control activities. Examples include sales reports, production schedules, inventory levels, and employee performance metrics.\n\n2. Tactical Information: This type of information is more summarized and aggregated compared to operational information. It provides insights into performance, trends, and patterns. It is used by tactical managers to evaluate performance and make medium-term decisions. Examples include sales forecasts, budget reports, market research data, and competitor analysis.\n\n3. Strategic Information: This type of information is high-level, future-oriented, and focuses on the external environment. It helps strategic managers analyze the industry, identify opportunities and threats, and make long-term decisions. Examples include industry reports, market trends analysis, economic forecasts, and SWOT analysis.\n\nIn summary, different levels of managers require different types of information for decision-making because they have distinct roles, responsibilities, and decision-making needs. Operational managers need detailed, real-time information for day-to-day operations, tactical managers need summarized information for medium-term planning, and strategic managers need high-level, future-oriented information for long-term strategic decision-making. By providing the right information to the right level of managers, organizations can enhance decision-making effectiveness and improve overall performance.	601	52f20d9f-5fa7-4c44-ab91-8b2bf7bb614e	1
