d4b06de0-f9d8-4d67-b171-26c1a8153b5d	assistant	2023-09-20 13:09:05.803342	Product and branding strategy is a crucial aspect of marketing that involves the development, positioning, and promotion of products or services in the market. Understanding the characteristics of products and how marketers classify them is essential for effective product and branding strategy. Here's an overview:\n\nCharacteristics of Products:\n1. Tangibility: Products can be physical objects that customers can touch, feel, and possess. Tangible products include items like clothing, electronics, or furniture.\n\n2. Intangibility: Some products are intangible and cannot be physically touched or possessed. These are often services, such as consulting, banking, or healthcare. Intangible products are experienced or consumed rather than owned.\n\n3. Durability: Products can have varying levels of durability. Some products are designed to last a long time, such as appliances or vehicles, while others are consumed or used up quickly, like food or personal care items.\n\n4. Perishability: Perishable products have a limited shelf life and can spoil or become unusable if not consumed within a certain timeframe. Examples include fresh produce, dairy products, or flowers.\n\n5. Variability: Products can have varying degrees of variability in terms of quality, features, or performance. This can be influenced by factors like manufacturing processes, materials used, or customization options.\n\n6. Inseparability: Inseparable products are often services that are produced and consumed simultaneously. The production and consumption of the service happen in real-time and cannot be separated. Examples include haircuts, live performances, or transportation services.\n\n7. Heterogeneity: Heterogeneous products have variations in quality or performance due to differences in production or delivery. This can be influenced by factors like human involvement, customization, or individual preferences.\n\nClassification of Products by Marketers:\n1. Consumer Products: Consumer products are intended for personal use or consumption by individuals. They can be further classified into four categories:\n   a. Convenience Products: These are low-priced, frequently purchased items that require minimal effort in purchasing. Examples include snacks, toiletries, or newspapers.\n   b. Shopping Products: Shopping products are higher-priced items that consumers compare and evaluate before making a purchase decision. Examples include clothing, electronics, or furniture.\n   c. Specialty Products: Specialty products are unique or exclusive items that consumers are willing to make a special effort to obtain. Examples include luxury goods, high-end jewelry, or limited edition collectibles.\n   d. Unsought Products: Unsought products are items that consumers may not actively seek out or be aware of until they have a need or problem. Examples include funeral services, insurance, or medical treatments.\n\n2. Industrial Products: Industrial products are purchased by organizations for use in their operations or for resale. They can be further classified into three categories:\n   a. Materials and Parts: These are raw materials, components, or sub-assemblies used in the production of other goods. Examples include steel, plastic, or electronic components.\n   b. Capital Goods: Capital goods are long-lasting, expensive items that are used in the production or operations of a business. Examples include machinery, equipment, or vehicles.\n   c. Supplies and Services: Supplies and services are consumable items or support services used in the day-to-day operations of a business. Examples include office supplies, maintenance services, or IT support.\n\nUnderstanding the characteristics and classifications of products helps marketers develop effective product and branding strategies. It enables them to tailor their marketing efforts, positioning, and messaging to meet the specific needs and preferences of their target market.	706	37122d89-a329-45b1-aff4-cbacc7074025	1
