59812d7f-0eb3-4e9c-b5cd-eca9b9ad48ab	assistant	2023-10-02 11:27:53.155166	Programmed decisions and unprogrammed decisions are two types of decisions that managers make in organizations. The main difference between them lies in the level of structure and routine involved in the decision-making process.\n\nProgrammed Decisions:\nProgrammed decisions are routine and repetitive decisions that are made in response to recurring situations. These decisions are typically based on established rules, procedures, and guidelines. They are made in situations where the problem and its solution are well-defined and have been encountered before. Programmed decisions are often automated or delegated to lower-level employees.\n\nCharacteristics of Programmed Decisions:\n1. Repetitive: Programmed decisions are made in response to recurring situations that have been encountered before.\n2. Routine: They follow established rules, procedures, and guidelines.\n3. Structured: The problem and its solution are well-defined and can be easily identified.\n4. Low-level management involvement: Programmed decisions are often automated or delegated to lower-level employees.\n5. Shorter decision-making time: Since the decision-making process is routine and well-defined, programmed decisions can be made quickly.\n\nExamples of Programmed Decisions:\n- Reordering office supplies when inventory reaches a certain level.\n- Approving employee vacation requests based on established policies.\n- Calculating payroll based on predetermined formulas.\n\nUnprogrammed Decisions:\nUnprogrammed decisions are non-routine and unique decisions that are made in response to new and complex situations. These decisions are made in situations where the problem is ambiguous, and there are no established rules or procedures to guide decision-making. Unprogrammed decisions require higher-level management involvement and a more extensive decision-making process.\n\nCharacteristics of Unprogrammed Decisions:\n1. Non-routine: Unprogrammed decisions are made in response to new and complex situations that have not been encountered before.\n2. Ambiguous: The problem is not well-defined, and there are no established rules or procedures to guide decision-making.\n3. Unstructured: The decision-making process requires creativity, analysis, and evaluation of various alternatives.\n4. High-level management involvement: Unprogrammed decisions require the expertise and experience of higher-level managers.\n5. Longer decision-making time: Due to the complexity and ambiguity, unprogrammed decisions take more time to analyze and evaluate alternatives.\n\nExamples of Unprogrammed Decisions:\n- Deciding to enter a new market or launch a new product.\n- Choosing a strategic direction for the organization.\n- Resolving a crisis or managing a major change in the organization.\n\nIn summary, programmed decisions are routine and repetitive decisions that follow established rules and procedures, while unprogrammed decisions are non-routine and unique decisions that require creativity and analysis. The level of structure, routine, and management involvement differs between the two types of decisions.	552	7ab62864-505c-466c-b4fd-a100751bb266	1
