da198680-93ac-4cd5-8cec-bf50e60b5f2e	assistant	2023-10-02 12:51:24.596686	A. To determine the profit-maximizing price and output, we need to find the quantity at which marginal revenue (MR) equals marginal cost (MC).\n\n1. Marginal Revenue (MR):\nMR is the change in total revenue resulting from a one-unit increase in quantity. For a monopolist, MR is not equal to the price (P) because the monopolist has market power and must lower the price to sell additional units. The formula for MR is:\n\nMR = d(TR) / d(Q)\n\nGiven that the total revenue (TR) is equal to the price (P) multiplied by the quantity (Q), we can express MR as:\n\nMR = d(PQ) / d(Q) = P + Q * d(P) / d(Q)\n\nIn this case, the demand function is P = 55 - 2Q. Taking the derivative of P with respect to Q, we get:\n\nd(P) / d(Q) = -2\n\nSubstituting this into the MR formula, we have:\n\nMR = P + Q * (-2) = 55 - 2Q - 2Q = 55 - 4Q\n\n2. Marginal Cost (MC):\nMC is the change in total cost resulting from a one-unit increase in quantity. The formula for MC is:\n\nMC = d(TC) / d(Q)\n\nGiven that the total cost (TC) is equal to 100 - 5Q + Q^2, we can express MC as:\n\nMC = d(100 - 5Q + Q^2) / d(Q) = -5 + 2Q\n\n3. Equating MR and MC:\nTo find the profit-maximizing quantity, we set MR equal to MC and solve for Q:\n\n55 - 4Q = -5 + 2Q\n\nSimplifying the equation, we get:\n\n6Q = 60\n\nQ = 10\n\n4. Determining the price:\nTo find the price corresponding to the profit-maximizing quantity, we substitute Q = 10 into the demand function:\n\nP = 55 - 2Q = 55 - 2(10) = 55 - 20 = 35\n\nTherefore, the profit-maximizing price is 35 and the output is 10.\n\nB. To compute the total profit, we need to subtract the total cost (TC) from the total revenue (TR). The formula for total revenue is:\n\nTR = P * Q\n\nSubstituting the values of P and Q, we have:\n\nTR = 35 * 10 = 350\n\nThe total cost (TC) is given by the equation:\n\nTC = 100 - 5Q + Q^2\n\nSubstituting the value of Q, we have:\n\nTC = 100 - 5(10) + 10^2 = 100 - 50 + 100 = 150\n\nTherefore, the total profit is:\n\nTotal Profit = TR - TC = 350 - 150 = 200\n\nC. To calculate the deadweight loss from monopoly power, we need to compare the monopolist's output level with the socially optimal output level under perfect competition.\n\nUnder perfect competition, the output level is determined where marginal cost (MC) equals the market price (P). In this case, the market price is given by the demand function:\n\nP = 55 - 2Q\n\nSetting MC equal to P, we have:\n\n-5 + 2Q = 55 - 2Q\n\n4Q = 60\n\nQ = 15\n\nSubstituting Q = 15 into the demand function, we find the socially optimal price:\n\nP = 55 - 2(15) = 55 - 30 = 25\n\nThe deadweight loss is the difference between the monopolist's output level (Q = 10) and the socially optimal output level (Q = 15), multiplied by the difference between the socially optimal price (P = 25) and the monopolist's price (P = 35):\n\nDeadweight Loss = (15 - 10) * (25 - 35) = 5 * (-10) = -50\n\nTherefore, the deadweight loss from monopoly power is 50.	865	80e2faaa-0d14-4442-864b-edc5e1825047	1
