eetings, engaging in collective bargaining, and participating in other union-related activities. The LRA recognizes the importance of allowing workers to actively participate in trade union activities without fear of reprisal or discrimination. This right ensures that PU members have the opportunity to contribute to the decision-making processes of the union and have their voices heard.\n\nMU, as the minority union, also enjoys certain organizational rights, although they may be subject to negotiation or agreement with the employer. These rights are aimed at ensuring that minority unions have a fair opportunity to represent their members and engage in trade union activities.\n\n1. Access to the workplace: MU has the right to access the premises of Dracula T (Pty) Ltd to communicate with its members and recruit new members, similar to PU. However, the extent of this access may be subject to negotiation or agreement between the employer and the union. The employer may have the right to impose reasonable restrictions on the access of MU representatives to the workplace, taking into account factors such as the size of the workplace and the need to maintain productivity.\n\n2. Deduction of union subscriptions: MU can also request Dracula T (Pty) Ltd to deduct union subscriptions from the salaries of its members who have given their consent. However, the employer may have the right to refuse this request if it would cause undue administrative burden or if there is an existing agreement with PU regarding subscription deductions. The employer may also have the right to negotiate the terms and conditions of the deduction of union subscriptions with MU, taking into account the specific circumstances of the workplace.\n\n3. Time off for trade union activities: MU members also have the right to take reasonable time off from work to participate in trade union activities, similar to PU. However, the employer may have the right to limit the number of representatives from MU who can take time off, based on the principle of representativeness. This means that the employer may consider factors such as the number of MU members in the workplace and the need to maintain productivity when granting time off for trade union activities.\n\nIt's important to note that the specific details and limitations of these organizational rights may vary depending on the collective bargaining agreements, workplace policies, and any other relevant agreements between the parties involved. The LRA provides a framework for these rights, but the actual implementation and interpretation may vary based on the specific circumstances of each workplace.	826	4fcf7891-7762-4d16-844a-675b9ebb64ca	1
