f2fe5fa4-2243-4f04-b32e-615a60b3a6f7	assistant	2023-10-09 09:39:25.667271	The correct answers are: fast-forward switching and fragment-free switching.\n\nFast-forward switching is a type of cut-through switching where the switch starts forwarding the frame as soon as it reads the destination MAC address. It does not perform any error checking or buffering, resulting in low latency but potentially allowing error frames to be forwarded.\n\nFragment-free switching is another type of cut-through switching that adds a small delay before forwarding the frame. This delay allows the switch to read the first 64 bytes of the frame, which is usually enough to detect most collision errors. This helps to prevent the forwarding of error frames, while still maintaining low latency.\n\nQOS switching, store-and-forward switching, and CRC switching are not examples of the cut-through switching method.	147	f10591ce-4e23-4872-ba24-258d2a3a226c	1
