c6932f88-1b47-4623-b7a8-892a550c3a7b	user	2023-10-16 09:06:14.265306	26. Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array\nEasy\n\n12885\n\n16999\n\nAdd to List\n\nShare\nGiven an integer array nums sorted in non-decreasing order, remove the duplicates in-place such that each unique element appears only once. The relative order of the elements should be kept the same. Then return the number of unique elements in nums.\n\nConsider the number of unique elements of nums to be k, to get accepted, you need to do the following things:\n\nChange the array nums such that the first k elements of nums contain the unique elements in the order they were present in nums initially. The remaining elements of nums are not important as well as the size of nums.\nReturn k.\nCustom Judge:\n\nThe judge will test your solution with the following code:\n\nint[] nums = [...]; // Input array\nint[] expectedNums = [...]; // The expected answer with correct length\n\nint k = removeDuplicates(nums); // Calls your implementation\n\nassert k == expectedNums.length;\nfor (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n    assert nums[i] == expectedNums[i];\n}	257	894927d2-a424-482d-b3f8-2e86be908ddb	1
abebf521-719a-4e77-9ae7-4d1b0c36b7d9	assistant	2023-10-16 09:06:14.265394	To solve this problem, we can use two pointers. One pointer, let's call it "i", will iterate through the array and keep track of the unique elements. Another pointer, let's call it "j", will be used to compare the current element with the previous element.\n\nHere's the step-by-step algorithm:\n\n1. Initialize "i" to 0 (representing the first element) and "j" to 1 (representing the second element).\n2. Iterate through the array starting from index 1 (since the first element is always unique).\n3. Compare the element at index "j" with the element at index "i". If they are equal, move "j" to the next element.\n4. If the elements are not equal, increment "i" by 1 and assign the element at index "j" to the element at index "i".\n5. Repeat steps 3 and 4 until "j" reaches the end of the array.\n6. Return "i + 1" as the number of unique elements.\n\nHere's the implementation in Java:\n\n```java\npublic int removeDuplicates(int[] nums) {\n    if (nums.length == 0) {\n        return 0;\n    }\n    \n    int i = 0;\n    for (int j = 1; j < nums.length; j++) {\n        if (nums[j] != nums[i]) {\n            i++;\n            nums[i] = nums[j];\n        }\n    }\n    \n    return i + 1;\n}\n```\n\nThis solution has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the input array.	334	894927d2-a424-482d-b3f8-2e86be908ddb	1
