ness and legality.\n\n2. Nationality Discrimination:\nCUGSA's demand for prioritizing South Africans when hiring raises concerns about potential discrimination based on nationality. In South Africa, the Constitution prohibits unfair discrimination on various grounds, including nationality, under Section 9(3) of the Bill of Rights.\n\nHowever, it is important to note that the Employment Services Act (ESA) allows for certain measures to promote employment opportunities for South African citizens. Section 9(2) of the ESA permits the Minister of Employment and Labour to issue regulations that provide for preferential treatment of South African citizens in employment matters, subject to certain conditions.\n\nThe fairness of prioritizing South Africans when hiring would depend on the specific circumstances and the extent to which it aligns with the objectives of the ESA and the broader employment equity framework. It is crucial to strike a balance between promoting employment opportunities for South Africans and avoiding unfair discrimination against non-South Africans.\n\nIn the case of Minister of Home Affairs v Watchenuka (2014), the South African Constitutional Court considered the issue of discrimination based on nationality. The court held that differentiation based on nationality can be justified if it is aimed at achieving a legitimate government objective and the means used to achieve that objective are reasonable and justifiable.\n\nConclusion:\nIn conclusion, the fairness of CUGSA's demands regarding retirement age and prioritization of South Africans when hiring depends on a careful assessment of the specific circumstances, the impact on various stakeholders, and the alignment with relevant legal provisions. The demands should be evaluated in light of the prohibition of unfair discrimination based on age and nationality, as well as the potential legitimate aims and fairness of the proposed measures.\n\nBased on the legal authority discussed, it is possible to