7c712c28-dc15-4c67-97cb-1511744d86ac	assistant	2023-10-19 15:01:46.632826	To find the longest common prefix string amongst an array of strings, you can use the following approach:\n\n1. Initialize a variable `prefix` with an empty string.\n\n2. If the input array `strs` is empty, return an empty string `prefix`.\n\n3. Iterate through the characters of the first string in the array `strs`.\n\n4. For each character at index `i`, compare it with the corresponding character at index `i` in the other strings in the array.\n\n5. If any character doesn't match or if the index `i` is greater than or equal to the length of any string in the array, return the `prefix`.\n\n6. If all characters at index `i` match in all strings, append the character to the `prefix` string.\n\n7. After iterating through all characters, return the `prefix` string.\n\nHere's the implementation of the function in JavaScript:\n\n```javascript\nfunction longestCommonPrefix(strs) {\n  let prefix = "";\n\n  if (strs.length === 0) {\n    return prefix;\n  }\n\n  for (let i = 0; i < strs[0].length; i++) {\n    const char = strs[0][i];\n\n    for (let j = 1; j < strs.length; j++) {\n      if (i >= strs[j].length || strs[j][i] !== char) {\n        return prefix;\n      }\n    }\n\n    prefix += char;\n  }\n\n  return prefix;\n}\n```\n\nYou can test the function with different inputs, such as:\n\n```javascript\nconsole.log(longestCommonPrefix(["flower", "flow", "flight"])); // Output: "fl"\nconsole.log(longestCommonPrefix(["dog", "racecar", "car"])); // Output: ""\nconsole.log(longestCommonPrefix(["apple", "application", "app"])); // Output: "app"\nconsole.log(longestCommonPrefix([])); // Output: ""\n```\n\nThe function should return the longest common prefix string amongst the array of strings.	414	d1d22482-ef2d-44fa-abac-992e5381a2af	1
