low the standard of care expected of a reasonable person.\n\nHowever, subsequent cases and legal developments have shown a growing recognition of the importance of subjective factors in assessing wrongfulness. For example, in the case of R v Cunningham [1957] 2 QB 396, the Court of Appeal held that the defendant's subjective knowledge and appreciation of the risk were relevant in determining whether he was guilty of maliciously administering a noxious substance.\n\nFurthermore, the adoption of statutes and legal principles that consider subjective factors, such as intention or recklessness, in various jurisdictions also reflects the shift towards a more subjective test for determining wrongfulness.\n\nIn conclusion, the debate between the traditional objective test and the newer subjective test for determining wrongfulness is ongoing. While the traditional test focuses on objective standards and the reasonable person standard, the subjective test takes into account the defendant's state of mind and individual circumstances. Relevant authorities, such as Donoghue v Stevenson and R v Cunningham, highlight the evolving nature of this debate and the recognition of the importance of subjective factors in assessing wrongfulness. Ultimately, the determination of the appropriate test for determining wrongfulness depends on the legal system and the specific context in which it is applied.	578	112fcac2-735b-47cb-a7bb-2f874b8369b5	1
