533df361-3400-4e2d-86d8-68b30d3fa2fb	assistant	2023-10-23 12:09:52.558986	Type 2 diabetes is a chronic condition that affects the way your body metabolizes sugar (glucose), which is the main source of fuel for your body. It occurs when your body becomes resistant to insulin or doesn't produce enough insulin to maintain normal blood sugar levels.\n\nHere are some key details about type 2 diabetes:\n\n1. Causes: The exact cause of type 2 diabetes is unknown, but several factors contribute to its development. These include genetics, obesity, sedentary lifestyle, poor diet, age, and certain medical conditions like high blood pressure and polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS).\n\n2. Insulin Resistance: In type 2 diabetes, the body's cells become resistant to the effects of insulin, a hormone produced by the pancreas. Insulin helps regulate blood sugar levels by allowing glucose to enter cells for energy. When cells become resistant, glucose builds up in the bloodstream, leading to high blood sugar levels.\n\n3. Symptoms: Common symptoms of type 2 diabetes include increased thirst, frequent urination, unexplained weight loss, fatigue, blurred vision, slow-healing sores or infections, and frequent infections (such as yeast infections).\n\n4. Diagnosis: Type 2 diabetes is typically diagnosed through blood tests that measure fasting blood sugar levels or through an oral glucose tolerance test. These tests help determine if your blood sugar levels are within the normal range or if you have prediabetes or diabetes.\n\n5. Treatment: Treatment for type 2 diabetes focuses on managing blood sugar levels and preventing complications. It often includes lifestyle changes such as adopting a healthy diet, regular exercise, weight loss (if overweight), and monitoring blood sugar levels. In some cases, oral medications or insulin injections may be prescribed to help control blood sugar levels.\n\n6. Complications: If left uncontrolled, type 2 diabetes can lead to various complications, including heart disease, stroke, kidney disease, nerve damage (neuropathy), eye problems (retinopathy), foot problems (diabetic foot), and skin conditions. Proper management and control of blood sugar levels can help reduce the risk of these complications.\n\n7. Prevention: While some risk factors for type 2 diabetes, such as genetics and age, cannot be changed, there are steps you can take to reduce your risk. These include maintaining a healthy weight, eating a balanced diet, engaging in regular physical activity, and avoiding tobacco use.\n\nIt's important to note that this information is a general overview, and it's always best to consult with a healthcare professional for personalized advice and guidance regarding type 2 diabetes.	520	7293429c-bb54-4d5f-9699-b747920b32f8	1
