d34980fb-6c09-4d57-8933-9a9f20258cd6	user	2023-10-27 07:07:32.70675	21 Which impact does adding a Layer 2 switch have on a network?\n21\nWhich impact does adding a Layer 2 switch have on a network?\n\nan increase in the size of the collision domain\n\nan increase in the number of network collisions\n\nan increase in the size of the broadcast domain\n\nan increase in the number of dropped frames\nNavigation Bar	975	eeb0bdd6-0ef8-4fd1-8de7-64ee014b8e3c	1
426b11ee-3288-46a9-a224-434903cddd4a	assistant	2023-10-27 07:07:32.706853	The correct answer is: an increase in the size of the broadcast domain.\n\nWhen a Layer 2 switch is added to a network, it creates a single broadcast domain for all the devices connected to it. A broadcast domain is a logical division of a network where broadcast traffic, such as ARP (Address Resolution Protocol) requests or DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) broadcasts, is contained. \n\nBy adding a Layer 2 switch, the number of devices within the broadcast domain increases, resulting in a larger broadcast domain. This means that broadcast traffic generated by any device within the broadcast domain will be received by all other devices in that domain. \n\nIt is important to note that a Layer 2 switch does not increase the size of the collision domain. Each port on a Layer 2 switch creates its own collision domain, which helps to reduce network collisions compared to using a hub.	175	eeb0bdd6-0ef8-4fd1-8de7-64ee014b8e3c	1
