8dc533de-6e23-47fe-92a3-6f21d6cabef6	assistant	2023-10-27 07:06:58.64672	The correct answer is: A hub extends a collision domain, and a switch divides collision domains.\n\nA hub is a simple networking device that operates at the physical layer of the OSI model. It receives incoming data packets and broadcasts them to all connected devices, extending the collision domain. This means that when one device transmits data, all other devices connected to the hub receive the data, potentially causing collisions.\n\nOn the other hand, a Layer 2 LAN switch operates at the data link layer of the OSI model. It receives incoming data packets and forwards them only to the intended destination device, based on the MAC address. This divides the collision domains, as each port on the switch is its own collision domain. This means that collisions are limited to the devices connected to a specific port, rather than being broadcasted to all devices like in a hub.\n\nTherefore, the significant difference between a hub and a Layer 2 LAN switch is that a hub extends a collision domain, while a switch divides collision domains.	200	eeb0bdd6-0ef8-4fd1-8de7-64ee014b8e3c	1
