82b55ff1-68a6-43b7-b7de-f578819311e4	assistant	2023-10-27 07:55:23.94334	The correct answer is:\n\nDHCP Snooping\n\nIP Source Guard\n\nBoth DHCP Snooping and IP Source Guard are Cisco solutions that help prevent DHCP starvation attacks.\n\nDHCP Snooping is a security feature that can be enabled on a Cisco switch to prevent unauthorized DHCP servers from providing IP addresses to clients. It works by inspecting DHCP messages and building a binding table of valid IP-MAC address pairs. If a DHCP server is detected on an untrusted port, DHCP Snooping can drop or rate-limit DHCP messages from that server, preventing DHCP starvation attacks.\n\nIP Source Guard is another security feature that can be enabled on a Cisco switch to prevent IP address spoofing and DHCP starvation attacks. It works by dynamically binding IP addresses to MAC addresses based on the DHCP snooping binding table or static IP-MAC bindings. IP Source Guard then uses these bindings to filter and restrict traffic on untrusted ports, preventing unauthorized IP address usage.\n\nThe Web Security Appliance, Dynamic ARP Inspection, and Port Security are not specifically designed to prevent DHCP starvation attacks.	208	eeb0bdd6-0ef8-4fd1-8de7-64ee014b8e3c	1
