583d45e0-0afc-4a5c-bfcb-71e7318b51b5	assistant	2023-10-27 13:27:10.764744	To solve this problem, we can iterate through the given sorted array `nums` and keep track of the start and end of each range. Whenever there is a gap between consecutive numbers, we add the current range to the result list and start a new range. Finally, we add the last range to the result list.\n\nHere is the step-by-step approach:\n\n1. Initialize an empty list `result` to store the ranges.\n2. Initialize variables `start` and `end` to keep track of the current range.\n3. Iterate through the elements `num` in the given sorted array `nums`.\n4. If `num` is consecutive to the previous number (`num == end + 1`), update the `end` of the current range to `num`.\n5. If `num` is not consecutive to the previous number, add the current range to the `result` list and start a new range with `start` and `end` both set to `num`.\n6. After the loop ends, add the last range to the `result` list.\n7. Convert each range in the `result` list to the required format ("a->b" if `start` != `end`, "a" if `start` == `end`).\n8. Return the `result` list.\n\nHere is the implementation in Python:\n\n```python\ndef summaryRanges(nums):\n    result = []\n    start = end = nums[0]\n    \n    for num in nums[1:]:\n        if num == end + 1:\n            end = num\n        else:\n            result.append((start, end))\n            start = end = num\n    \n    result.append((start, end))\n    \n    return [str(start) if start == end else str(start) + "->" + str(end) for start, end in result]\n```\n\nLet's test the function with the given example:\n\n```python\nnums = [0,1,2,4,5,7]\nprint(summaryRanges(nums))\n```\n\nOutput:\n```\n["0->2", "4->5", "7"]\n```\n\nThe smallest sorted list of ranges that cover all the numbers in the array `[0,1,2,4,5,7]` is `["0->2", "4->5", "7"]`.	472	171333b5-29ed-43a0-a61d-22f82e129893	1
