96087caa-1700-4405-b4c7-6474aa4e0e89	assistant	2023-11-16 10:24:57.363372	Isoquants represent different combinations of inputs that can produce the same level of output. The slope of an isoquant indicates the rate at which one input can be substituted for another while keeping the output constant.\n\nIsoquants must slope downward as long as both inputs are productive because of the principle of diminishing marginal rate of technical substitution (MRTS). This principle states that as more of one input is substituted for another while keeping output constant, the marginal rate of substitution between the inputs will decrease.\n\nIn other words, as you increase the quantity of one input while decreasing the quantity of another input, there will be a point where the additional output gained from increasing the quantity of one input will be less than the output lost from decreasing the quantity of the other input. This is due to the diminishing returns to scale, which means that the marginal productivity of an input decreases as more of it is used in the production process.\n\nAs a result, isoquants slope downward because the trade-off between inputs becomes less favorable as you move along the isoquant. This downward slope reflects the decreasing MRTS and the diminishing returns to scale in production.	227	7398a7cf-2a10-43e5-aaf0-b14f75ac86bb	1
