50bbdd0b-1690-4257-81e8-e75f65567483	assistant	2023-11-18 17:32:33.981834	The claim of bloodletting was based on the ancient medical theory of balancing the body's humors. According to this theory, the human body contained four primary fluids or humors: blood, phlegm, yellow bile, and black bile. It was believed that an imbalance of these humors caused illness, and bloodletting was thought to restore the balance.\n\nThe practice of bloodletting involved intentionally removing blood from the body, usually by making incisions or using leeches. The idea was that by removing excess blood, the body's humors would be brought back into equilibrium, thus alleviating the illness.\n\nBloodletting was believed to have various benefits, including reducing fever, relieving inflammation, and improving overall health. It was commonly used to treat a wide range of conditions, from infections and fevers to mental disorders and even chronic illnesses.\n\nHowever, the medical understanding of the time was based on flawed theories, and bloodletting often did more harm than good. Excessive blood loss could weaken the patient and lead to further complications, especially in cases where the underlying illness was already severe.\n\nIt's important to note that bloodletting was a widely accepted medical practice during George Washington's time, and it was not until the late 19th century that its use began to decline with the advancement of modern medicine.	269	4007d903-8949-4e40-b84a-c03f43c3ecb2	1
