c71f1548-f465-4c86-9e23-b85ff375ea88	assistant	2023-11-18 19:32:45.180287	The primary sources of data for ELT teachers' professional development in Addis Ababa Public selected secondary schools can include:\n\n1. Surveys: Surveys can be conducted among ELT teachers to gather information about their professional development needs, preferences, and experiences. These surveys can be administered online or in-person and can include both closed-ended and open-ended questions.\n\n2. Interviews: Interviews with ELT teachers can provide in-depth insights into their professional development experiences, challenges, and successes. These interviews can be conducted one-on-one or in focus groups and can be audio-recorded or transcribed for analysis.\n\n3. Classroom observations: Observations of ELT teachers' classroom practices can provide valuable data on their teaching methods, strategies, and use of instructional materials. These observations can be conducted by trained observers or peers and can be documented through written notes or video recordings.\n\n4. Reflective journals: ELT teachers can maintain reflective journals where they document their thoughts, reflections, and experiences related to their professional development. These journals can provide rich qualitative data on their growth and learning over time.\n\nSecondary sources of data for ELT teachers' professional development in Addis Ababa Public selected secondary schools can include:\n\n1. Existing research studies: Previous research studies conducted on ELT teachers' professional development in similar contexts can provide valuable insights and findings that can be used to inform the current study.\n\n2. Policy documents: Policy documents related to education and professional development in Ethiopia, such as government guidelines or curriculum frameworks, can provide information on the official expectations and requirements for ELT teachers' professional development.\n\n3. School records: School records, such as attendance registers for professional development workshops or training programs, can provide data on the participation and engagement of ELT teachers in professional development activities.\n\n4. Teacher portfolios: Teacher portfolios, which may include documentation of professional development activities, certificates, or evidence of classroom practices, can serve as secondary sources of data on ELT teachers' professional development.\n\nIt is important to note that the specific primary and secondary sources of data may vary depending on the research design and objectives of the study on ELT teachers' professional development in Addis Ababa Public selected secondary schools.	437	a3ba61b8-b3e6-486e-8404-6fa42844900d	1
