rms, paper-based surveys, or in-person interviews, depending on the feasibility and preferences of the participants.\n\n6. Data analysis: Analyze the collected data using statistical software. Calculate descriptive statistics (e.g., mean, standard deviation) to understand the overall teacher beliefs or attitudes towards professional development. You can also conduct inferential statistics (e.g., t-tests, ANOVA) to explore potential relationships between teacher beliefs or attitudes and other variables of interest.\n\n7. Interpretation: Interpret the findings based on the data analysis. Examine the teachers' beliefs or attitudes towards professional development, identify any patterns or differences, and relate them to the current practices, perceptions, and challenges of EFL/ELT professional development in government secondary schools in Addis Ababa.\n\n8. Reporting: Present your findings in a clear and organized manner, using tables, charts, and narratives. Discuss the implications of the teacher beliefs or attitudes towards professional development for improving practices in government secondary schools. Make recommendations for enhancing EFL/ELT professional development based on the insights gained from the data analysis.\n\nRemember to obtain ethical approval for your research and ensure the confidentiality and anonymity of the participants throughout the process.	602	6307de4a-ad7b-42c1-b723-790e828ee010	1
