could limit the ways in which their biases and personal feelings could influence their decision.\nFayol's administrative theory, also known as Fayolism, consists of 14 principles of management that outline the tasks managers are expected to perform. These principles continue to be used today, but their implementation varies depending on a company's use of technology and culture. For instance, compensation systems may differ in societies that stress individual outcomes compared to those focused on group outcomes\nFayol’s 14 Principles of Management are:1. Division of Work,2. Authority 3. Discipline,4. Unity of Command,5. Unity of Direction,6. Subordination of Individual Interest,7. Remuneration,8. Centralization,9. Scalar Chain,10. Order,11. Equity,12. Stability of Tenure of Personnel,13. Initiative—Employees should be given the necessary level of freedom to create and carry out plans,14. Esprit de Corps.In addition to the 14 principles, Fayol identified the five functions of management Planning,Organizing, Staffing, Controlling,Directing	1737	aacfac56-6746-442f-ab89-d618d0567f57	1
