7cc3f94f-edfa-430a-a610-1ba46add3826	assistant	2023-11-26 17:53:55.809328	Taylor's approach, known as scientific management, was centered around the belief that workers should be trained in the most efficient methods for performing their tasks, while managers closely monitored their work to ensure adherence to these methods. This approach aimed to address issues such as high levels of soldiering (deliberate underperformance) due to fear of reduced pay or dissatisfaction with the work system. To tackle these challenges, Taylor proposed several solutions.\n\nFirstly, he implemented time and motion studies to standardize work activities. This involved breaking down tasks into individual motions, eliminating unnecessary movements, selecting the best approach for completing the job, and timing each motion without accounting for delays. The goal was to determine the amount of work a worker could complete in a day or month based on available resources.\n\nSecondly, Taylor advocated for the adoption of a uniform method for routine tasks. This aimed to align the work process with the capabilities and skills of the worker, ensuring a better fit between the individual and the job requirements.\n\nAdditionally, Taylor introduced the concept of functional foremanship, which involved scientifically selecting the most suitable worker for a specific job based on their skills and potential for learning. This approach aimed to optimize the allocation of tasks to workers, ensuring that each individual was assigned to a role that aligned with their abilities.\n\nLastly, Taylor emphasized the importance of individual incentives in determining appropriate wages or salaries. By implementing a piece-rate pay system, where workers were compensated based on the number of units produced, Taylor sought to motivate employees and reward their productivity.\n\nOverall, Taylor's scientific management approach sought to improve efficiency and productivity by training workers in the best methods, standardizing work processes, optimizing task allocation, and implementing individual incentives.	338	aacfac56-6746-442f-ab89-d618d0567f57	1
