ther treatment A gives better results at a 5% level of significance, we can perform a hypothesis test. The null hypothesis (H0) is that there is no difference between the means of treatment A and treatment B, and the alternative hypothesis (Ha) is that treatment A gives better results.\n\nH0: μA - μB = 0\nHa: μA - μB > 0\n\nWe can use a t-test to test this hypothesis. With a sample size of 5 and 5 degrees of freedom, the critical t-value for a 5% level of significance (one-tailed test) is approximately 1.833.\n\nt = (point estimate - hypothesized difference) / standard error\n\nHypothesized difference = 0\nStandard error = 1.4 (calculated earlier)\nPoint estimate = 3 (calculated earlier)\n\nt = (3 - 0) / 1.4 ≈ 2.14\n\nSince the calculated t-value (2.14) is greater than the critical t-value (1.833), we reject the null hypothesis.\n\nTherefore, at a 5% level of significance, we can conclude that treatment A gives better results.	836	fa511f66-e710-4638-bd0a-2b77774e737a	1
