d catches the worm." - Being proactive and starting early can lead to success.\n21. "The grass is always greener on the other side." - People tend to think others have it better than them, when in reality, they may not.\n22. "The squeaky wheel gets the grease." - Those who speak up or make their needs known are more likely to receive attention or assistance.\n23. "Two wrongs don't make a right." - Responding to a wrongdoing with another wrongdoing doesn't solve the problem.\n24. "When in Rome, do as the Romans do." - Adapt to the customs and behavior of the place you are in.\n25. "You can't judge a book by its cover." - You can't make assumptions about someone or something based solely on appearance.\n26. "A penny saved is a penny earned." - Saving money is just as valuable as earning it.\n27. "A picture is worth a thousand words." - Visual representation can convey more meaning than words alone.\n28. "Beauty is in the eye of the beholder." - Perception of beauty varies from person to person.\n29. "Every man is the architect of his own fortune." - Each individual is responsible for their own success or failure.\n30. "Give a man a fish and you feed him for a day; teach a man to fish and you feed him for a lifetime." - It's better to teach someone a skill or provide them with the means to support themselves rather than just giving them temporary assistance.\n31. "Great minds think alike." - Intelligent or creative people often have similar ideas or opinions.\n32. "Laughter is the best medicine." - Humor and joy can have a positive impact on one's well-being.\n33. "Money can't buy happiness." - Material wealth does not guarantee true happiness or fulfillment.\n34. "One man's trash is another man's treasure." - What one person may consider worthless, another person may find valuable.\n35. "Out of sight, out of mind." - When something or someone is no longer visible or present, they are easily forgotten.\n36. "Practice makes perfect." - Repeatedly doing something improve