e1664ab5-0c5b-4c91-bdf5-295682b82088	assistant	2023-12-23 19:48:30.824974	To determine the time complexity of the given code snippet, let's analyze the number of operations performed as a function of the input size n.\n\nThe outer loop runs from i = 0 to i = n-1, which means it executes n times.\n\nFor each iteration of the outer loop, the inner loop runs from j = 0 to j = i-1. In the first iteration, the inner loop runs 0 times. In the second iteration, it runs 1 time. In the third iteration, it runs 2 times, and so on.\n\nSo, the total number of times the inner loop runs can be calculated as the sum of the first (n-1) natural numbers: 0 + 1 + 2 + ... + (n-2) = (n-2)(n-1)/2.\n\nWithin the inner loop, the statement "Sum += j" is executed for each iteration of the inner loop. This statement performs a constant-time operation.\n\nTherefore, the total number of operations can be calculated as the product of the number of times the inner loop runs and the number of operations within the inner loop:\n\nt(n) = (n-2)(n-1)/2 * 1\n\nSimplifying the expression:\n\nt(n) = (n^2 - 3n + 2)/2\n\nRemoving constant factors and lower-order terms, we get:\n\nt(n) = O(n^2)\n\nSo, the time complexity of the given code snippet is O(n^2), indicating that the number of operations grows quadratically with the input size.	324	0fc1e0da-17df-4fbd-b99d-c9f494277d5d	1
