211289dc-729c-4956-b49e-fb9988983873	assistant	2023-12-29 08:35:19.129855	In an operating system, base and limit registers are used for memory management and protection. \n\nThe base register holds the starting address of a memory segment, while the limit register specifies the size of the segment. Together, they define the range of memory that a process can access.\n\nWhen a process is executing, the base register is used to calculate the actual physical address of a memory location by adding the base address to the logical address provided by the process. This allows the process to access the correct memory location within its allocated segment.\n\nThe limit register is used to ensure that a process does not exceed its allocated memory space. Before accessing a memory location, the operating system checks if the logical address falls within the range defined by the base and limit registers. If the address is outside the valid range, a memory access violation occurs, preventing unauthorized access to other processes' memory.\n\nBy using base and limit registers, the operating system can enforce memory protection and prevent processes from accessing memory outside their allocated segments. This helps ensure the stability and security of the system by preventing processes from interfering with each other's memory.	219	92726c51-fe4a-458d-8d19-04a5f77c699d	1
