d45e183b-cb40-4dff-900b-f8f04281a2d4	assistant	2023-12-31 03:36:04.115739	CPU burst refers to the period of time during which a process or task is actively using the CPU for computation or execution. It represents the duration for which the CPU is engaged in executing instructions for a particular process. CPU bursts can vary in length depending on the nature of the task and the characteristics of the workload.\n\nOn the other hand, I/O burst refers to the period of time during which a process is waiting for input/output (I/O) operations to be completed. During an I/O burst, the process is not actively using the CPU but is instead waiting for data to be read from or written to an external device, such as a hard disk or network interface.\n\nIn most systems, processes alternate between CPU bursts and I/O bursts. They perform computation during CPU bursts and then wait for I/O operations to complete during I/O bursts. This pattern is common in many applications, as processes often need to interact with external devices or access data from secondary storage.\n\nUnderstanding the characteristics of CPU bursts and I/O bursts is crucial for effective process scheduling and resource management in operating systems. Schedulers aim to optimize the utilization of CPU and I/O resources by efficiently scheduling processes based on their burst characteristics and priorities.	240	6daec260-7647-4967-b79f-07579e7b6b7e	1
