24983664-3204-42aa-bd57-e5faef91722d	assistant	2024-01-16 08:02:50.553156	Title: Changing Active Voice to Passive Voice: A Comprehensive Guide\n\nIntroduction:\nGood morning/afternoon/evening, ladies and gentlemen. Today, I am here to present a short guide on changing active voice to passive voice. Understanding this fundamental concept is crucial for effective communication and writing. We will explore the formulas for converting active voice sentences into passive voice sentences in the present, past, and future tenses. So, let's dive in!\n\nI. Present Tense:\nIn the present tense, the formula for changing active voice to passive voice is as follows:\n\nSubject + am/is/are + past participle + (by + agent)\n\nExample:\nActive Voice: The cat chases the mouse.\nPassive Voice: The mouse is chased by the cat.\n\nNote: The agent (the doer of the action) is optional and can be included using the preposition "by."\n\nII. Past Tense:\nIn the past tense, the formula for changing active voice to passive voice is slightly different:\n\nSubject + was/were + past participle + (by + agent)\n\nExample:\nActive Voice: The teacher taught the students.\nPassive Voice: The students were taught by the teacher.\n\nNote: The verb "to be" is conjugated in the past tense (was/were) and followed by the past participle of the main verb.\n\nIII. Future Tense:\nIn the future tense, the formula for changing active voice to passive voice is as follows:\n\nSubject + will/shall + be + past participle + (by + agent)\n\nExample:\nActive Voice: The company will launch a new product.\nPassive Voice: A new product will be launched by the company.\n\nNote: The auxiliary verb "will/shall" is used to indicate the future tense, followed by "be" and the past participle of the main verb. The agent can be included using the preposition "by."\n\nConclusion:\nIn conclusion, understanding how to change active voice to passive voice is essential for effective communication and writing. By following the formulas 