efb64c09-94a4-4684-9afd-a9a88e76e06e	In mixed methods research, a researcher collects both numeric information (for example, through CLOSED-RESPONSE ITEMs on QUESTIONNAIREs) and text (from FACE-TO-FACE INTERVIEWs, picture descriptions, and so on) to better answer a study’s research questions. The term mixing implies that the data or the findings are integrated and/or connected at one or several points within the study. The goal of mixed methods research is not to replace qualitative or quantitative approaches but, rather, to combine both approaches in creative ways that utilize the strengths of each within a single study. By mixing methods in ways that minimize weaknesses or ensure that the weaknesses of one approach do not overlap significantly with the weaknesses of another, the study is strengthened.	2023-12-02 08:37:07.725553	f	\N	0	703a5185-2e98-470e-90c4-a24557de800d	\N	630ca76a-9c8a-4808-a77f-ab38fe9a209f	2023-12-02 08:47:01.247905
